        "content": "Restrictions operate through more than formal newspaper bans. In its report on the first half of 2025, the Defending Free Flow of Information organization documented threatening calls, pressure to remove posts, interference with journalists’ communications, and judicial proceedings. These are findings from an organization monitoring press suppression, rather than a government statistical census. They provide evidence of reporting constraints without measuring the entire media economy. [s5]\n\nAccess conditions can also change abruptly. Reporters Without Borders, an international press-freedom organization, reported a communications blackout in January 2026, difficulty contacting independent newsrooms, and the suspension of Ham-Mihan. Its account describes that episode; it does not establish which websites or print editions were operating at the October research cutoff. A temporarily unreachable website cannot alone prove permanent closure. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exploring-the-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Following the right branch",
        "content": "The pages below separate institutions that gather and distribute news from newspapers with their own editions and editorial identities. National newspapers are particularly useful for studying changes in public argument over time, while agency profiles help identify the institutional origin of frequently repeated reports. Historical archives support both approaches, provided the publication date and original context remain visible. [s2][s3][s4]\n\nThis introduction is strongest on agencies, national newspapers, and documented reporting restrictions. It does not yet provide an adequately verified account of provincial, specialist, economic, or technology journalism. Those branches require their own sources rather than assumptions drawn from Tehran’s national press.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
