    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian News Agencies: IRNA, Mehr and ISNA",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to selected Iranian news agencies, their publishing connections, language services, and verification limits."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "agency-identities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Start with the agency’s identity",
        "content": "Agency names often travel with a report into other publications. Understanding the institution named in that attribution is a useful first step, but a directory profile cannot substitute for checking the underlying report. The Organization of Asia-Pacific News Agencies, or OANA, provides institutional profiles for IRNA and Mehr that establish their names and describe parts of their publishing operations. These are institutional descriptions rather than independent assessments of editorial performance. [s1][s2]\n\nIRNA stands for Islamic Republic News Agency. OANA traces its origins to Pars News Agency in 1934 and dates its renaming to 1982. Its profile also describes newspaper publishing through the Iran Cultural and Press Institute. The important distinction is between the agency and the publications associated with it: a newspaper bearing a related institutional identity is still a particular publication, with its own edition and content. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "publishing-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connections between agencies and newspapers",
        "content": "Mehr provides another example of an agency connected to a newspaper. OANA’s profile describes Mehr Media Group as comprising Mehr News Agency and the Tehran Times. That documented connection helps readers recognize that apparently separate publication brands can belong to a shared organizational structure. The profile’s promotional claims about reach should not be treated as audited audience evidence. [s2]\n\nThese examples also caution against treating 'news agency' and 'newspaper' as mutually exclusive corporate categories. The relevant questions concern the specific organization and product: who issued the original item, which publication reproduced it, and whether additional reporting was added. The verified profiles support the existence of publishing connections; they do not establish the complete editorial decision-making process inside either group. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "language-and-archives",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "English-language material and its limits",
        "content": "The Iranian Students’ News Agency is commonly abbreviated ISNA. A research abstract by Mohammad Kamel and Hadi Sadoghi-Yazdi describes an English-language news corpus assembled from ISNA material. This establishes that English ISNA reporting was available for that research, rather than demonstrating that its English output represented the full Persian service. Only the abstract was inspected here; detailed corpus findings are therefore outside this page’s evidence base. [s3]\n\nAn English article is best approached as a specific editorial product. Readers should record its date, attributed speaker, and original publisher, and avoid assuming equivalence with another language edition unless both versions have been checked. The ISNA research identifies a language-specific corpus, while OANA separately identifies agency websites and institutional connections. Neither source supplies a comprehensive comparison of translated and original output. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-and-current-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A selective introduction",
        "content": "Availability is another dimension of the agency landscape. In its January 2026 reporting on Iran’s communications blackout, Reporters Without Borders noted that some state-aligned information services remained accessible while independent newsrooms were difficult to reach. This is evidence about unequal access during a documented episode, rather than a permanent classification of every agency’s availability or reliability. [s4]\n\nThe profiles here are deliberately selective. A publication-ready national overview also requires verified histories, institutional affiliations, and current operating information for other major agencies, including Fars and Tasnim. Their omission from the detailed profiles should not be read as a judgment about their importance.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
