    "description": "Iran’s national newspapers preserve a record of changing journalistic styles, institutional relationships, and political debate. Their titles must be read in historical context because ownership, editorial direction, and permission to publish have changed over time.",
    "tags": ["National newspapers", "Kayhan", "Ayandegan", "Iran newspaper", "Press history", "Archives"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:07:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:07:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "National Newspapers in Iran: Titles, History and Archives",
      "description": "Explore selected Iranian national newspapers, historical changes in ownership and editorial identity, and sources for reading original editions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "national-press",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Newspapers as institutions and historical records",
        "content": "Iran’s national newspaper history is more than a succession of titles. It includes competition over journalistic style, changes in ownership, and disputes over what could be published. Kayhan illustrates the importance of period: Encyclopaedia Iranica describes its development under Mostafa Mesbahzadeh before the revolution and its later publication under the patronage of the Islamic Republic’s supreme leader. A title’s longevity therefore cannot establish an unchanged editorial identity. [s1]\n\nThe Tehran Kayhan must also be distinguished from Kayhan of London. Iranica identifies the latter as a separate weekly founded in exile in 1984. Confusing the two would merge publications with different institutional histories. This distinction matters when searching archives, identifying an editorial, or attributing a contemporary statement. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ayandegan",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ayandegan and competition over reporting",
        "content": "Ayandegan first appeared in Tehran on 16 December 1967. Its founders sought a morning newspaper with a more substantial approach to reporting and commentary, challenging the established position of Ettelaat and Kayhan. Iranica’s account emphasizes both its innovations and the political controls within which it worked. Describing its liberalizing ambitions does not imply that it operated in an unrestricted press system. [s2]\n\nThe same account distinguishes influence from circulation: Ayandegan did not match the two large evening papers in sales, yet carried weight among political elites. It is a useful historical example of why circulation alone cannot measure a newspaper’s role in public debate. Its subsequent closure belongs to the broader contraction of the press after the revolution. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "changing-institutions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Revolution and later publishing arrangements",
        "content": "The revolutionary opening did not produce a lasting unrestricted newspaper market. Hossein Shahidi’s historical account describes closures, including Ayandegan, and the state takeover of Ettelaat and Kayhan by August 1979. It also records the emergence of publications associated with competing political actors. A newspaper’s institutional position must consequently be located in time rather than projected backward or forward from a single period. [s3]\n\nThe daily Iran represents a different publishing connection. Iranica dates its establishment to January 1995 and identifies it as a newspaper published by IRNA, the Islamic Republic News Agency. This distinguishes Iran, the newspaper title, from both the country and the agency itself. The historical account explains its association with government perspectives, but cannot establish its precise editorial arrangements at the present cutoff. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recent-disruption",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recent restrictions need dated descriptions",
        "content": "Reporters Without Borders reported that Ham-Mihan was suspended in January 2026 and that several newspaper websites were inaccessible from abroad during the communications blackout. These are dated findings. They should not be expanded into a claim that all affected newspapers permanently ceased printing or remained inaccessible throughout the year. Current operating status requires later, publication-specific evidence. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-original-editions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading original editions",
        "content": "For historical exploration, the University of Manchester Library’s Nashriyah collection offers digitized newspapers and periodicals from several major political turning points. Reading an original edition helps preserve the relationship between headlines, commentary, advertisements, and surrounding reports. The library describes a selected collection, so gaps in its holdings should not be interpreted as proof that a title or issue never existed. [s6]\n\nThe most useful reading habit is to retain the publication name and issue date alongside every excerpt. Compare the article’s wording with its attributed source, and separate an editorial position from a reported event. National newspapers can illuminate competing interpretations of Iranian history without any single title standing for the country as a whole. [s1][s2][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s6"]
      }
    ],
